   "description": "Scientists borrowed a trick from code-breakers and found a hidden pulsar in public NASA data using about 10 hours of computing. The previous best method had used the equivalent of 600 years and missed it. NASA says the new method finds pulsars about a million times faster.\n\nIn this episode:\n0:00 600 years vs. 10 hours\n0:20 What a pulsar is\n0:43 Why gamma-ray pulsars are so hard to find\n1:28 Hundreds of computer-years per pulsar\n1:42 The code-breaking trick: shortest vector in a lattice\n2:14 PSR J1622−7204, found\n2:53 Why open NASA data mattered\n3:10 Next target: pulsars with a companion star\n\nSources:\n- NASA, \"NASA Science Data Powers New Method to Discover Hard-to-Find Pulsars\" (Nov 19, 2026): https://science.nasa.gov/open-science/fermi-data-pulsar-discovery/\n- Study: Pook-Kolb, Gazith, Zackay, Clark et al., Physical Review Letters (published Nov 19, 2026)\n- NASA SVS, \"Fermi's 15-year View of the Gamma-Ray Sky\": https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14976\n- NASA SVS, \"Fermi Discovers Youngest Millisecond Pulsar\" (neutron star facts): https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10858\n\nFootage and imagery: NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center (Conceptual Image Lab, Scientific Visualization Studio): https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14881 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/20413 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/10858 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/13156 , https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/20417 ; sky maps NASA/DOE/Fermi LAT Collaboration. Public domain; NASA does not endorse this video. Animations are artist's visualizations, not photographs. Lattice and comparison graphics: NEXORA.\nNarration: synthetic voice (ElevenLabs).\n\n#NASA #Pulsar #Fermi",
